00:00:26.616 --> 00:00:28.000
PROFESSOR: Hi, everyone.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.550
We're going to get started.

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:32.970
So today, we're--
it's another lab day.

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:35.550
So we're going to
split up, and half

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:37.240
is going to be outside again.

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:38.882
So hopefully, you
brought your coats.

00:00:38.882 --> 00:00:40.590
If you haven't, you
should start learning

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:42.070
that's a good thing to do.

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:45.450
And then the other half
in the shop, finishing up

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:47.190
the woodwork you did.

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:51.120
And then we're also just going
to do some metal work and PVC

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:55.770
work just so you get familiar
with all the tools in the shop.

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:57.510
For the pumps, I
think [INAUDIBLE]

00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:58.320
is going to talk a little bit.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:00:59.362
I just wanted to mention.

00:00:59.362 --> 00:01:03.030
Someone asked, like,
is a foot pump always

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:04.650
better than a hand pump?

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:06.420
And the answer is no.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:08.850
There's a variety of reasons
why you might want a hand

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:10.920
pump over a foot pump.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.780
One of them that's
a little subtle

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:17.670
is that in some cultures,
it's not acceptable for women

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:20.110
to stand over water sources.

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:23.400
And so you can't use a
foot pump as a woman.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.725
And so if the woman needs
to be able to do the work,

00:01:25.725 --> 00:01:26.850
she has to use a hand pump.

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:29.670
So there's many places
where foot pumps have not

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:31.830
worked because of that
reason, and hand pumps have

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:32.670
worked out great.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:35.550
So there's-- I mean, I think
we'll talk about some other

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:38.670
reasons, but there's a lot of
variety in what's going to work

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:40.290
better, where, and why.

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:42.628
So yeah, it's not--

00:01:42.628 --> 00:01:44.170
while you guys were
calculating power

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:45.240
and that's what we're
a little obsessed

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.640
about in this class, that's
not the only thing you

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.260
have to worry about
is maximum power.

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:56.508
PROFESSOR: OK, so addressing
the rest of the pump questions,

00:01:56.508 --> 00:01:58.800
when you're actually trying
to decide what kind of pump

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:00.840
to implement in a
specific site, there

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.190
are a few things you need
to take into consideration.

00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:05.047
First, you need to
figure out how much water

00:02:05.047 --> 00:02:05.880
is going to be used.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.280
If you're going to pump
bucket of water a day,

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:11.753
building an expensive rocket
pump doesn't really make sense,

00:02:11.753 --> 00:02:13.170
and a hand pump
will do the trick.

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:17.040
Whereas if you're trying to
irrigate your entire field,

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.643
then maybe a cement pump is a
better alternative to the hand

00:02:19.643 --> 00:02:21.060
pump because you
don't want to sit

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:25.230
there pumping with your hand
for hours and hours and hours.

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:29.550
That being said, the other
thing is that different pumps

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:31.260
can pump different
amounts of water

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:35.640
and can pump over
different pressure heads.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:39.750
So the cement pump
is a diaphragm pump,

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:45.230
and I think it can pump about
5 to 7 meters deep only.

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:48.560
So it's not really used to
pump water from a low water

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:50.600
source like a borehole
or something like that

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:53.000
because it just won't
be able to do that.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:59.150
A little bit about how it
works is you have two valves.

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:02.930
And then you have this sort of--

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:06.310
it's called a diaphragm.

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:09.680
And then you have
another valve here.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.340
And so what happens
is this thing

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:15.190
can change anywhere between
this side and this side

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:16.490
when you step on it.

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:19.810
And so when you
step down, you're

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:21.220
constricting the volume here.

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:26.110
And water is going to
get pushed out of here

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:29.260
into this from your outlet.

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:33.220
And then when you release it and
you push up, this valve closes.

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:35.920
And then there's a
pressure difference in here

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:38.630
or negative pressure in here,
which will open this valve

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:39.380
and pull water in.

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:41.922
So it works pretty much the same
way with two one way valves.

00:03:41.922 --> 00:03:44.290
But the thing is that
you can't generate

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:47.030
that big a pressure difference.

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:50.860
And that's why you
can't pump over that,

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:52.690
or you can't pump that deep.

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:54.820
And so that's another
one of the considerations

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:56.200
you have to take into account.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:58.750
I think when you want to pump
from boreholes or something,

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:04.030
you want to get somewhere
around 50-ish meters deep.

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:06.490
I know the hand pump that
we went over the other day

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:10.150
pumps somewhere between
20 and 40 meters deep,

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:14.590
and the treadle pump, which you
may have seen in other places,

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:15.137
it's similar.

00:04:15.137 --> 00:04:16.720
But instead of rocking
back and forth,

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:19.550
you lift up your legs up
and down kind of like this.

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:22.089
And it's the one that
IDE uses, and it's mostly

00:04:22.089 --> 00:04:23.440
used for irrigation of fields.

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:30.350
And that one, I know, pumps
only seven meters deep.

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:33.380
And I lost my--

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:35.380
cool.

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:36.010
Yes.

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:37.593
AUDIENCE: Have any
of these pumps been

00:04:37.593 --> 00:04:41.680
used for, like,
filtrations and [INAUDIBLE]

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:45.630
for just carrying
[INAUDIBLE] water?

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:46.602
Can you put a filter--

00:04:46.602 --> 00:04:48.810
it seems really rudimentary,
but can you put a filter

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:51.520
at the end of one of them, kind
of like a valve, and then just

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:55.190
collect [INAUDIBLE]?

00:04:55.190 --> 00:04:58.190
PROFESSOR: I've seen people
use pumps with filters on them,

00:04:58.190 --> 00:05:01.132
generally larger pore filters.

00:05:01.132 --> 00:05:02.840
But the problem with
that is you normally

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:05.180
have to replace the
filter every so often,

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:08.215
and you don't want an
internal part of the pump

00:05:08.215 --> 00:05:10.340
because it gets tedious to
replace it all the time.

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:13.340
But I've seen people put, like,
just cloth filters over the end

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:16.750
to get rid of turbidity.

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:20.380
PROFESSOR: Great, so because
a pump is pushing out

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:24.370
a relatively high pressure and
filters tend to work slowly,

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:27.730
it's rare that you can just
put a filter at the end.

00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:30.550
So typically, while it
would be great to just

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:32.920
have point of use
water purification

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:35.710
and cleaning, apart
from turbidity factors,

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:37.960
it's hard to do
that effectively.

00:05:46.390 --> 00:05:47.920
PROFESSOR: So,
yeah, so those are,

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:51.300
I think, the two biggest
questions from last week's lab.

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:52.800
Do any of you have
more questions

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:55.960
about last week's lab?

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:57.280
Cool.

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:00.718
So a little bit about what
we're going to do today--

00:06:00.718 --> 00:06:02.260
the objectives are
we're going to try

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:07.522
to learn about how to get energy
from solar panels, PV panels.

00:06:07.522 --> 00:06:09.230
So they're going to
be working with them.

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:12.610
We have one that's the size and
one that's maybe three times

00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:14.980
the size of this.

00:06:14.980 --> 00:06:19.220
So we're going to take these
outside and hook them up.

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:21.670
And then we're going
to learn about little

00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:24.100
bit about the theory
behind not actually

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:25.750
how the PV panel works.

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:27.075
We'll go into that later.

00:06:27.075 --> 00:06:28.450
But this is more
about how to use

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:31.420
the PV panel and some
applications that

00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:32.685
come from that.

00:06:32.685 --> 00:06:34.810
We're going to learn how
to hook it up to a battery

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:37.855
because that's one of the
most common uses, I think.

00:06:37.855 --> 00:06:39.730
And we're going to look
at some of the design

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:40.600
considerations
you're going to have

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:42.340
to take into account
when you look

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:43.705
at designing a solar system.

00:06:46.250 --> 00:06:48.310
And so that's sort of
one half of the lab,

00:06:48.310 --> 00:06:50.980
and the other half is going to
be working in the shop again.

00:06:50.980 --> 00:06:52.990
And you're going to learn more
about fabrication techniques.

00:06:52.990 --> 00:06:54.823
But this time, instead
of working with wood,

00:06:54.823 --> 00:06:57.520
we're going to look at other
materials like metal and PVC

00:06:57.520 --> 00:06:58.330
and sheet metal.

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:02.290
Cool.

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:05.860
So where's my sheet?

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:10.480
OK, so when you're trying
to get energy, when you're

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:15.940
trying to get
power from the sun,

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:18.700
you basically have
your sun, and then you

00:07:18.700 --> 00:07:23.650
have the surface of the
Earth and rays coming in.

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:27.700
Do any of you know how much best
case, when the sun is shining,

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:30.580
there's no clouds blocking
the way, what the best case

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:36.361
power you can get from the sun
is in watts per meter squared?

00:07:36.361 --> 00:07:39.190
AUDIENCE: 1,000.

00:07:39.190 --> 00:07:46.480
PROFESSOR: Yeah,
it's about 1,000.

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:49.570
Now at the beginning of
the first class we sat in,

00:07:49.570 --> 00:07:53.680
we tried calculating the average
daily electrical consumption

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:56.560
or average electrical
consumption per year

00:07:56.560 --> 00:07:59.070
for people living in
the developing world.

00:07:59.070 --> 00:08:02.400
And based off of
those numbers, we

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:08.900
can approximate that
people in Nicaragua

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:14.990
use about 1 kilowatt
hours a day,

00:08:14.990 --> 00:08:24.290
and then people living in Haiti
use about 0.1 kilowatt hours

00:08:24.290 --> 00:08:26.390
of electricity a day.

00:08:28.862 --> 00:08:30.320
So if we're looking
at solar, we're

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:37.328
looking to power all of these or
the one kilowatt hour, the 0.01

00:08:37.328 --> 00:08:39.620
kilowatt hours, that people
are using in the developing

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:41.480
world using this energy.

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:46.280
How much surface area would
we need to harness the power?

00:08:46.280 --> 00:08:48.680
So we can walk
through it, right?

00:08:48.680 --> 00:09:02.710
And we get-- and then you
know that a day has 24 hours.

00:09:02.710 --> 00:09:05.846
So you can multiply that out.

00:09:05.846 --> 00:09:09.262
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] 24 hours.

00:09:09.262 --> 00:09:13.180
AUDIENCE: The sun doesn't
shine for 24 hours, though.

00:09:13.180 --> 00:09:17.470
PROFESSOR: Yeah, it's true.

00:09:17.470 --> 00:09:20.360
I will get to that in a second.

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:31.320
So you get about that much
surface area where you need.

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:33.890
So if you could ideally
harness the power of the sun

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:39.710
at this much power for 24
hours a day for one day,

00:09:39.710 --> 00:09:43.010
you would only need
a collector that's

00:09:43.010 --> 00:09:46.470
0.04 meters squared,
which is pretty tiny,

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:47.970
which seems like a
really good idea.

00:09:47.970 --> 00:09:49.790
But there are a
lot of reasons why

00:09:49.790 --> 00:09:51.890
this is not the right
way of figuring out

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:54.050
how much power you need.

00:09:54.050 --> 00:09:57.380
So like said, for
example, the sun does--

00:09:57.380 --> 00:09:59.160
how far down can you
guys read it again?

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:00.343
Is it about here?

00:10:00.343 --> 00:10:01.385
AUDIENCE: A little lower.

00:10:01.385 --> 00:10:02.335
There

00:10:02.335 --> 00:10:05.610
PROFESSOR: There, OK.

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:06.110
So yeah.

00:10:08.750 --> 00:10:10.663
So let's brainstorm
some of these ideas

00:10:10.663 --> 00:10:11.705
or some of these reasons.

00:10:21.291 --> 00:10:23.624
AUDIENCE: The maximum we know
is 1,000 watts [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:23.624 --> 00:10:26.942
equals the sun
times [INAUDIBLE]..

00:10:36.008 --> 00:10:38.591
AUDIENCE: Some people are going
to want more than one kilowatt

00:10:38.591 --> 00:10:40.052
hour [INAUDIBLE].

00:10:53.353 --> 00:10:54.020
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

00:10:54.020 --> 00:10:55.850
AUDIENCE: So to
find demand, it's

00:10:55.850 --> 00:11:00.590
not always a exact same
match, so there's no storage.

00:11:00.590 --> 00:11:02.780
It was-- because right now,
there's no storage yet.

00:11:02.780 --> 00:11:07.697
So you might need fluctuations
from 0 to 1 for [INAUDIBLE]..

00:11:07.697 --> 00:11:08.280
PROFESSOR: OK.

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:10.204
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:11:13.090 --> 00:11:17.282
AUDIENCE: You get, like, max,
about 30% from [INAUDIBLE]..

00:11:25.220 --> 00:11:27.695
AUDIENCE: That efficiency goes
down even more as the panels

00:11:27.695 --> 00:11:30.929
age and also if it gets any
scratches or dirt on them.

00:11:35.570 --> 00:11:38.540
PROFESSOR: OK, so let's start
looking at some of these

00:11:38.540 --> 00:11:47.050
and quantifying some of these
constraints we have now.

00:11:47.050 --> 00:11:49.040
So we can't do this anymore.

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:51.040
Let's still work with
these numbers as ballparks

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:52.990
because, I mean, we're
only really concerned

00:11:52.990 --> 00:11:56.005
about the order of magnitude
estimations, really, for this.

00:12:01.980 --> 00:12:04.680
So efficiency of PV--

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:06.630
and max, you get about 30%.

00:12:06.630 --> 00:12:10.530
But I think that's
the laboratory cutting

00:12:10.530 --> 00:12:13.290
[INAUDIBLE] technology
that is really expensive.

00:12:13.290 --> 00:12:20.980
With commercially available
PV, it's somewhere

00:12:20.980 --> 00:12:25.330
between 8% and 18% is what
you're going to get right now.

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:34.463
Yeah, we're not going
to look at that one yet.

00:12:34.463 --> 00:12:35.880
So this one-- about
how many hours

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:38.190
do you think should
we approximate the sun

00:12:38.190 --> 00:12:41.550
to be up a day?

00:12:41.550 --> 00:12:44.678
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:12:46.142 --> 00:12:49.070
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:54.700 --> 00:12:55.610
AUDIENCE: 10 hours.

00:12:55.610 --> 00:12:56.980
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:56.980 --> 00:12:59.860
PROFESSOR: OK, so
let's go 10 hours.

00:12:59.860 --> 00:13:01.820
I think that's still a bit high.

00:13:01.820 --> 00:13:04.037
I think somewhere
around 8 is better.

00:13:04.037 --> 00:13:08.600
AUDIENCE: Yeah,
assuming equatorial.

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:12.160
PROFESSOR: Let's go eight hours.

00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:15.410
And then storage is
also a big factor

00:13:15.410 --> 00:13:21.040
that you have a huge amount of
energy lost in storage as well.

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:24.588
This is one of the
reasons why just energy

00:13:24.588 --> 00:13:27.130
is a problem because we have no
really good way of storing it

00:13:27.130 --> 00:13:29.170
yet.

00:13:29.170 --> 00:13:31.420
So generally, when
you are trying

00:13:31.420 --> 00:13:33.095
to get power from
your solar panel,

00:13:33.095 --> 00:13:34.720
you're going to try
to charge something

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:36.220
with it, like a battery.

00:13:36.220 --> 00:13:38.410
And when you charge
a battery with it,

00:13:38.410 --> 00:13:42.203
you have built-in losses.

00:13:42.203 --> 00:13:43.870
And the efficiency
of transferring power

00:13:43.870 --> 00:13:46.360
from a solar panel to
a battery without using

00:13:46.360 --> 00:13:51.880
any voltage regulation or
anything because that has

00:13:51.880 --> 00:13:55.860
energy loss too is about 75%.

00:14:04.090 --> 00:14:06.220
And so now that you've
stored your energy,

00:14:06.220 --> 00:14:07.600
what sort of
applications are you

00:14:07.600 --> 00:14:11.770
going to try to power
off of this battery?

00:14:11.770 --> 00:14:13.916
[INAUDIBLE]

00:14:15.310 --> 00:14:17.100
AUDIENCE: Like small appliances.

00:14:17.100 --> 00:14:18.725
PROFESSOR: Yeah, and
what kind of power

00:14:18.725 --> 00:14:19.950
do small appliances take?

00:14:19.950 --> 00:14:20.880
Are you going to--

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:21.855
AC?

00:14:21.855 --> 00:14:23.880
So you're going to
have to convert this

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:25.980
because when you have
power stored in a battery,

00:14:25.980 --> 00:14:26.940
it's stored as DC.

00:14:26.940 --> 00:14:30.450
So you have to convert
it to AC again.

00:14:30.450 --> 00:14:34.860
So you have this DC to
AC conversion, which

00:14:34.860 --> 00:14:37.590
also has a loss in efficiency.

00:14:37.590 --> 00:14:40.440
And then we can say
that's about 75% too.

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:46.470
It varies a lot depending on
how expensive your inverter is

00:14:46.470 --> 00:14:49.980
and how you're matching your
loads with that inverter.

00:14:49.980 --> 00:14:54.810
So it goes anywhere between,
like, 50% and 90%, I'd say.

00:14:54.810 --> 00:14:58.570
But sticking to
75% is reasonable.

00:14:58.570 --> 00:15:01.950
So now that we have
all of these numbers,

00:15:01.950 --> 00:15:06.700
we can reevaluate
our calculations.

00:15:06.700 --> 00:15:11.520
So we have one kilowatt
hours still for Nicaragua.

00:15:11.520 --> 00:15:33.565
And now we're dividing
it by times 0.75.

00:15:33.565 --> 00:15:35.232
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:15:35.232 --> 00:15:37.565
PROFESSOR: Do-- I think we
want them in the denominator.

00:15:37.565 --> 00:15:39.720
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:41.470
PROFESSOR: Yeah, let's
do this because I'm

00:15:41.470 --> 00:15:42.940
running out of space.

00:15:42.940 --> 00:15:45.514
And let's just say
that that's 10%.

00:15:45.514 --> 00:15:52.693
AUDIENCE: 1 over the lifetime
of the [INAUDIBLE] efficiency.

00:15:52.693 --> 00:15:53.360
PROFESSOR: Sure.

00:16:00.330 --> 00:16:07.310
OK, and does someone have a
calculator and want to do this?

00:16:07.310 --> 00:16:08.750
I can do it on my computer too.

00:16:11.200 --> 00:16:11.700
Let's race.

00:16:20.368 --> 00:16:21.370
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:16:21.370 --> 00:16:22.162
AUDIENCE: Go, Jess.

00:16:22.162 --> 00:16:23.350
Go.

00:16:23.350 --> 00:16:28.110
AUDIENCE: 0.002777778.

00:16:28.110 --> 00:16:30.460
[INAUDIBLE]

00:16:30.460 --> 00:16:33.900
PROFESSOR: I think that's--

00:16:33.900 --> 00:16:36.020
well, it's kilowatts
divided by that.

00:16:36.020 --> 00:16:42.340
So it's actually going
to be 2.7 or whatever.

00:16:42.340 --> 00:16:46.240
It's going to be about
three meters squared.

00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:47.400
AUDIENCE: Yeah, 2.7.

00:16:47.400 --> 00:16:52.030
PROFESSOR: Yeah, which
is suddenly a lot larger

00:16:52.030 --> 00:16:54.650
than what we had before.

00:16:54.650 --> 00:16:56.560
And this is still
assuming that this is

00:16:56.560 --> 00:16:58.790
going to be best case scenario.

00:16:58.790 --> 00:17:02.680
And so if we assume
that the sun is

00:17:02.680 --> 00:17:05.290
going to be shining
bright every day,

00:17:05.290 --> 00:17:07.630
we're going to have to have
a solar panel at least three

00:17:07.630 --> 00:17:09.354
meters or three square meters.

00:17:12.770 --> 00:17:15.220
And then we have to think
about other things too

00:17:15.220 --> 00:17:17.859
when we're dealing
with PV usage,

00:17:17.859 --> 00:17:19.930
like what is the
power being used for

00:17:19.930 --> 00:17:21.965
and how reliable
does it need to be.

00:17:21.965 --> 00:17:26.500
So what are some
places where we could

00:17:26.500 --> 00:17:29.230
think about installing
solar panels where

00:17:29.230 --> 00:17:30.850
it wouldn't need to
be reliable and it

00:17:30.850 --> 00:17:34.558
would need to be very reliable?

00:17:34.558 --> 00:17:35.542
AUDIENCE: Hospitals.

00:17:46.147 --> 00:17:46.730
PROFESSOR: OK.

00:17:49.430 --> 00:17:56.150
I guess in a non-reliable, maybe
a home where you don't-- if you

00:17:56.150 --> 00:17:59.210
have a house that's not using
refrigeration and they're using

00:17:59.210 --> 00:18:01.970
lighting, if they don't
have electricity for one day

00:18:01.970 --> 00:18:04.220
or something like that, it's
not the end of the world.

00:18:10.840 --> 00:18:13.210
But when you're
looking at designing

00:18:13.210 --> 00:18:18.340
a solar photovoltaic system
for a community partner,

00:18:18.340 --> 00:18:20.530
you need to be asking
these sorts of questions.

00:18:20.530 --> 00:18:22.300
Like, how long can
you go without power?

00:18:22.300 --> 00:18:25.190
For a hospital, if you can't
go a day without power,

00:18:25.190 --> 00:18:29.140
what do you do if there's no
sun for a week if it's cloudy?

00:18:29.140 --> 00:18:33.390
You can't have
people who are on--

00:18:33.390 --> 00:18:35.140
you just can't have
that for the hospital.

00:18:35.140 --> 00:18:37.420
So you need to build a
more robust system that

00:18:37.420 --> 00:18:41.200
can draw more power and store
it better whereas for housing,

00:18:41.200 --> 00:18:43.260
maybe a couple of days if not--

00:18:43.260 --> 00:18:45.650
where maybe you can afford
to go without electricity

00:18:45.650 --> 00:18:46.525
for a couple of days.

00:18:52.930 --> 00:18:53.920
So yeah.

00:18:53.920 --> 00:18:59.110
And then does anyone know
what the cost of photovoltaics

00:18:59.110 --> 00:19:00.130
is as well?

00:19:06.948 --> 00:19:11.818
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] I
think the best things are

00:19:11.818 --> 00:19:15.740
around $1 [INAUDIBLE].

00:19:21.143 --> 00:19:23.060
PROFESSOR: Yeah, when
we were looking this up,

00:19:23.060 --> 00:19:26.990
we found a pretty big range
for how expensive they were.

00:19:26.990 --> 00:19:28.970
I think some most
of the sources were

00:19:28.970 --> 00:19:32.210
saying it was around $3
to $5 for the cheapest

00:19:32.210 --> 00:19:38.720
PV we could find, but it's
still about that order for watt.

00:19:38.720 --> 00:19:41.270
And so I guess the thing you
should take away from this

00:19:41.270 --> 00:19:44.540
is a lot of times, people look
at lighting and just say--

00:19:44.540 --> 00:19:47.960
or electricity and
just assume that solar

00:19:47.960 --> 00:19:50.000
is like a miraculous
solution that's

00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:52.100
going to solve
everything, and you really

00:19:52.100 --> 00:19:54.380
don't want to think
about it as that.

00:19:54.380 --> 00:19:56.090
You want to actually
think about all

00:19:56.090 --> 00:19:58.985
of the considerations that
go into creating a system.

00:20:03.610 --> 00:20:08.220
So now I'll show you--

00:20:08.220 --> 00:20:12.900
this is a solar panel
that we got for D lab.

00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:15.720
We have two of these.

00:20:15.720 --> 00:20:22.070
Basically, you have a positive
and ground coming out of it.

00:20:22.070 --> 00:20:24.260
So you can hook it up to a load.

00:20:24.260 --> 00:20:26.560
But if you want to hook
it up to a battery,

00:20:26.560 --> 00:20:28.698
you have to somehow
regulate the voltage

00:20:28.698 --> 00:20:31.240
before you're doing that because
if you plug this in directly

00:20:31.240 --> 00:20:33.880
to-- well, what'll happen if
you're trying to charge a 12

00:20:33.880 --> 00:20:40.900
volt battery and the solar panel
is outputting about 20 volts.

00:20:40.900 --> 00:20:42.947
And it's a lead acid
battery, like a car battery

00:20:42.947 --> 00:20:44.155
that you're trying to charge.

00:20:48.720 --> 00:20:51.870
You'll probably-- yeah,
it'll probably explode.

00:20:51.870 --> 00:20:54.630
I think if you drive
too much voltage

00:20:54.630 --> 00:20:56.993
or shoot through a
car battery, you're

00:20:56.993 --> 00:20:58.410
going to end up
producing hydrogen

00:20:58.410 --> 00:21:02.920
and oxygen as byproducts,
and it's very flammable.

00:21:02.920 --> 00:21:05.250
And if there's a spark,
then it explodes.

00:21:05.250 --> 00:21:08.640
What about the opposite case
where your solar panel is

00:21:08.640 --> 00:21:10.830
outputting eight volts
or something like that

00:21:10.830 --> 00:21:14.490
or six volts, and your battery
is trying to take 12 volts?

00:21:14.490 --> 00:21:16.602
AUDIENCE: It'll never
charge more than that.

00:21:16.602 --> 00:21:18.060
PROFESSOR: Yeah,
more so than that,

00:21:18.060 --> 00:21:20.018
though, not that it's
not only going to charge,

00:21:20.018 --> 00:21:22.420
but your solar panel is going
to discharge your battery.

00:21:22.420 --> 00:21:23.920
So you're going to
do the opposite.

00:21:23.920 --> 00:21:27.420
So you need to build in some
sort of regulation for it.

00:21:27.420 --> 00:21:29.820
You're going to have to have
a voltage regulator that

00:21:29.820 --> 00:21:33.360
makes sure that you're
outputting the right amount

00:21:33.360 --> 00:21:34.632
of voltage or--

00:21:34.632 --> 00:21:36.090
and then you also
need to make sure

00:21:36.090 --> 00:21:38.790
that you have a diode
in there somewhere so

00:21:38.790 --> 00:21:43.680
that electricity doesn't back
flow and drain the battery.

00:21:43.680 --> 00:21:48.180
Luckily for us, instead of
having to design and build

00:21:48.180 --> 00:21:50.710
all of these things, there are
companies that do it for us.

00:21:50.710 --> 00:21:54.270
So we have a couple of
these solar controllers.

00:21:58.350 --> 00:22:00.150
And so they have
this stuff built

00:22:00.150 --> 00:22:02.070
in so you can hook
a solar panel up

00:22:02.070 --> 00:22:06.360
to two ends of this and your
battery up to the other two.

00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:08.390
And then you can charge
a battery from that.

00:22:08.390 --> 00:22:10.390
And this is just-- there
are two different ones.

00:22:10.390 --> 00:22:13.800
This one takes-- this one
you can put a load directly

00:22:13.800 --> 00:22:16.330
onto as well.

00:22:16.330 --> 00:22:20.610
And the last thing I
was going to talk about

00:22:20.610 --> 00:22:23.760
are resistors and
potentiometers.

00:22:23.760 --> 00:22:26.550
Do you guys know how-- have
guys seen potentiometers before?

00:22:29.220 --> 00:22:30.480
OK, yeah.

00:22:30.480 --> 00:22:34.500
So what a potentiometer is,
it's a variable resistor.

00:22:34.500 --> 00:22:39.160
And so this thing
is a resistant--

00:22:39.160 --> 00:22:42.150
the thing has uniform
resistance that changes linearly

00:22:42.150 --> 00:22:43.480
with how you turn this.

00:22:43.480 --> 00:22:47.370
So if you're over
here, you effectively

00:22:47.370 --> 00:22:49.020
have a resistor
that's this long.

00:22:49.020 --> 00:22:50.970
And then when you
open it to this far,

00:22:50.970 --> 00:22:52.830
you get a resistor
that's twice as long.

00:22:52.830 --> 00:22:56.790
And so you can change the
resistance anywhere from 0 to--

00:22:56.790 --> 00:22:59.315
this one's rated at 500 ohms.

00:22:59.315 --> 00:23:00.690
And it's kind of
like what we did

00:23:00.690 --> 00:23:07.350
the other day with
the resistance

00:23:07.350 --> 00:23:11.070
is equal to rho l over a.

00:23:11.070 --> 00:23:16.247
So you're just changing l
and getting more resistance.

00:23:16.247 --> 00:23:18.330
So the way you use these,
you're going to use them

00:23:18.330 --> 00:23:19.690
later on in the lab.

00:23:19.690 --> 00:23:22.380
So I just thought I'd
explain it right now--

00:23:22.380 --> 00:23:24.960
is you hook up to
these two terminals.

00:23:24.960 --> 00:23:27.150
If you hook up across
here, you're just

00:23:27.150 --> 00:23:29.430
going to get the full 500 ohms.

00:23:29.430 --> 00:23:33.270
And if you hook up to here,
as you sweep this across it,

00:23:33.270 --> 00:23:34.620
your resistance will go up.

00:23:38.670 --> 00:23:40.680
And when your guys are
doing the lab, remember.

00:23:40.680 --> 00:23:42.850
I wrote it as a note, and
I'll explain it again, there.

00:23:42.850 --> 00:23:44.475
But remember that
when you're measuring

00:23:44.475 --> 00:23:46.680
the resistance of this,
you want to disconnect it

00:23:46.680 --> 00:23:50.130
from the solar panel
because if you don't, you're

00:23:50.130 --> 00:23:51.570
going to fry the volt meter.

00:23:51.570 --> 00:23:54.720
And it's not-- yeah, we don't
want you to fry volt meters

00:23:54.720 --> 00:23:58.020
because they're expensive and
we only have two right now.

00:24:01.480 --> 00:24:06.207
So do you have any
questions about what

00:24:06.207 --> 00:24:07.290
we talked about right now?

00:24:10.070 --> 00:24:12.950
OK, so we could split
into two groups.

00:24:15.770 --> 00:24:16.850
PROFESSOR: Yeah, OK.

00:24:16.850 --> 00:24:20.370
So everyone can sit down.

00:24:20.370 --> 00:24:21.545
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

